    "summary": "Compute ordered ranking across partitions with ties handled with or without gaps.",
    "theory": "Window ranking functions calculate position based on `ORDER BY`:\n- `RANK()`: Assigns the same rank to ties, but leaves gaps in subsequent ranks (1, 2, 2, 4).\n- `DENSE_RANK()`: Assigns the same rank to ties, with NO gaps in subsequent ranks (1, 2, 2, 3).",
    "pro_tip": "Use `DENSE_RANK()` when finding the Nth highest value (e.g. 2nd highest salary), regardless of how many employees share the top salary.",
    "expected_output": "Ranked leaderboard of employees ordered by salary with rank and dense_rank.",
    "sql_file": "42 SQL Query RANK() AND DENSE_RANK().sql",
    "html_file": "notes/topic-42-rank-and-dense-rank.html",
    "slug": "topic-42-rank-and-dense-rank",
    "raw_sql": "--Rank() & dense_rank()\n\nuse google\n\nselect * from employee\n\nselect *, rank() over(order by salary desc) as Rank_salary from employee\n\nselect *, rank() over(order by name) as Name_rank from employee\n\nSELECT *, dense_rank() OVER (ORDER BY salary DESC) AS dense_rank_sal from employee\n\n--we can only put rank and dense_rank over a column which can be sorted.\nSELECT *, RANK() over (Order by salary desc) as Rank_sal, dense_rank() over(order by salary desc) as dense_rank_sal from employee"
